# Line endings are a cross-platform correctness problem, not a style preference.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows default) a clone on Windows rewrites every
# text file to CRLF in the working tree. That is harmless for Python, and fatal for a shell
# script: `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally named
# "bash\r", and the script dies with "bad interpreter" the moment the tree reaches macOS or
# Linux. Pinning eol=lf on the executable text files removes the whole class of failure.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything with a shebang, plus the formats where a stray CR changes meaning.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
scripts/ollie text eol=lf

# Windows shells are the mirror image: cmd.exe reads a .bat line at a time and a lone LF
# can split a command in the wrong place, so these keep CRLF everywhere.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Never touched. `text=auto` would otherwise corrupt anything it misdetects as text.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.pdf       binary
*.epub      binary
*.gguf      binary
*.safetensors binary
*.db        binary
*.dll       binary
*.dylib     binary
*.so        binary
